{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G1m5Q2WKn9BAriSmvu31qW","type":"tool_result","content":"158\tAn inconclusive verdict is informative only relative to the effects the design could have resolved. Three properties of the protocol, all computed from its constants and the reported interval (Table~\\ref{tab:resolution}), limit that resolution.\n159\t% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json#full]]\n160\t\n161\t\\emph{Bounded dynamic range.} In this balanced design the slope is a fixed linear contrast of the width-wise mean log-delays $m_d=\\tfrac14\\sum_s\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$:\n162\t\\begin{equation}\n163\t\\beta=\\sum_{d} w_d\\,m_d,\\qquad (w_{16},\\dots,w_{256})=(-0.2,\\,-0.1,\\,0,\\,0.1,\\,0.2),\n164\t\\end{equation}\n165\tthat is, $\\beta=0.2\\,(m_{256}-m_{16})+0.1\\,(m_{128}-m_{32})$; the middle width carries no weight, and the verdict depends only on how the two widest models compare with the two narrowest. Because every $m_d\\in[\\ln 250,\\ln 10{,}000]$, $|\\beta|\\le 0.3\\ln 40=1.107$, attained by a step profile; a delay falling log-linearly from the cap at $d=16$ to the floor at $d=256$ would give $0.922$. The interval has width $0.939$ and covers $42\\%$ of the attainable range $[-1.107,1.107]$. Its lower arm extends $0.565$ below the point estimate and its upper arm $0.374$ above.\n166\t\n167\t\\emph{Distance to a supported verdict.} Keeping the upper arm of the interval unchanged, a verdict of supported would have required a point estimate below $-0.374$. The reference slope ($-0.368$) is slightly above that value; with the same upper arm it would give an upper endpoint of $+0.006$, still straddling zero. This is arithmetic on the observed spread, not a forecast: the spread of a different point estimate would differ.\n168\t\n169\t\\emph{A coarse bootstrap.} With four seeds there are only $\\binom{7}{4}=35$ distinct multisets of seeds to resample, and a fraction $4/4^4=1/64$ of draws consist of a single seed repeated four times. The $2{,}000$ resamples therefore estimate a discrete distribution with at most $35$ support points; the percentile endpoints $-0.585$ and $0.354$ should be read as coarse, and the coverage of an interval built from four clusters is not guaranteed to be nominal.\n170\t\n171\t\\begin{table}[t]\n172\t\\centering\n173\t\\caption{Quantities derived from the protocol constants and the reported interval (not additional measurements).}\n174\t\\label{tab:resolution}\n175\t\\small\n176\t\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n177\t\\toprule\n178\tQuantity & Value \\\\\n179\t\\midrule\n180\tDelay floor / cap (steps) & $250$ / $10{,}000$ \\\\\n181\tLargest attainable $|\\text{slope}|$ (step profile) & $1.107$ \\\\\n182\tSlope of a log-linear cap-to-floor profile & $0.922$ \\\\\n183\tInterval width & $0.939$ \\\\\n184\tInterval width as fraction of $[-1.107, 1.107]$ & $0.42$ \\\\\n185\tInterval arms below / above the point estimate & $0.565$ / $0.374$ \\\\\n186\tPer-doubling delay factor: point [interval] & $0.980$ [$0.557$, $1.425$] \\\\\n187\t$16\\to256$ delay factor: point [interval] & $0.924$ [$0.096$, $4.12$] \\\\\n188\t$16\\to256$ delay factor at the reference slope & $0.230$ \\\\\n189\tDistinct bootstrap seed multisets & $35$ \\\\\n190\t\\bottomrule\n191\t\\end{tabular}\n192\t\\end{table}\n193\t\n194\t\\subsection{Censoring}\n195\tForty percent of the $20$ runs ($8$ runs) were censored at the step limit and entered the regression with the cap value $10{,}000$. Censoring has two consequences. First, because censored delays are replaced by the largest permitted value, any real dependence whose long-delay end is censored is compressed, so the fitted slope is biased toward zero when long delays concentrate at one end of the width range. Second, \\emph{censored} here covers two different events, held-out accuracy never reaching $0.99$ and training accuracy never reaching $0.99$; the retained artifacts do not say which occurred or at which widths, so they cannot separate delayed generalization from failure to fit. The score in Table~\\ref{tab:metrics} offers a threshold-free view: averaged over widths and seeds, held-out accuracy integrated over the $40$ checkpoints is $0.729$, so the runs on average generalize to a substantial degree within the window, but this aggregate does not locate where in the width range that happens.\n196\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.censored_fraction]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#n_censored_runs]]\n197\t\n198\t\\subsection{Seed-level variability}\n199\tThe four seeds differ considerably in the score (Table~\\ref{tab:seeds}), from $0.607$ to $0.860$ with mean $0.729$ and sample standard deviation $0.104$. We report this as variability across seeds, not as a performance comparison: each seed's score averages five widths, and a single seed supplies one run at every width. That the seed-to-seed spread is large relative to the quantities of interest is consistent with the wide interval for the slope, although the artifacts do not allow a variance decomposition across widths.\n200\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#seed_score_sd]]\n201\t\n202\t\\begin{table}[t]\n203\t\\centering\n204\t\\caption{Seed-level records from the sealed evaluation. Score: mean over the five widths of the held-out-accuracy AUC. Wall-clock time is summed over all episodes with that seed; seed 201 also includes the two control runs.}\n205\t\\label{tab:seeds}\n206\t\\small\n207\t\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n208\t\\toprule\n209\tSeed & Score & Wall-clock (s) \\\\\n210\t\\midrule\n211\t201 & $0.607$ & $2{,}468$ \\\\\n212\t202 & $0.733$ & $1{,}894$ \\\\\n213\t203 & $0.717$ & $2{,}062$ \\\\\n214\t204 & $0.860$ & $1{,}943$ \\\\\n215\t\\midrule\n216\tMean $\\pm$ s.d. & $0.729 \\pm 0.104$ & \\\\\n217\t\\bottomrule\n218\t\\end{tabular}\n219\t\\end{table}\n220\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n221\t\n222\t\\subsection{Cross-check against the reference trainer}\n223\tThe reference trainer, run on seed $201$ only, gives a slope of $-0.368$, a fitted $16\\to256$ delay factor of $0.230$, and lies inside the interval, $23\\%$ of the way from its lower endpoint to its upper. The evaluated model's point estimate is $0.348$ larger (less negative). We draw no conclusion about this difference: the reference slope is one seed with no uncertainty, five points define it, and the evaluated model differs from the reference in three simultaneous ways (Section~\\ref{sec:model}). The comparison also does not establish that the multipliers weaken the width dependence: that would require both variants to be run with several seeds under the same protocol, which was not done.\n224\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.reference_delay_log_slope]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#reference_position_in_ci_fraction_from_low]]\n225\t\n226\t\\subsection{Validity checks and development-plane context}\n227\tThe two controls stayed far below their ceiling (maximum held-out accuracy $0.012$ against a ceiling of $0.10$), no episodes were missing, and the evaluator reports a completed, valid evaluation. These checks support the integrity of the measurement. They do not bear on the width question, and a valid measurement can be inconclusive.\n228\t\n229\tFor context, three configurations that differ cumulatively by the three modifications of Section~\\ref{sec:model} were evaluated on the separate development plane with two seeds each, with scores $0.798$, $0.828$, and $0.850$ and $4$, $6$, and $4$ of $6$ runs censored (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}). Differences between configuration means are no larger than the seed-to-seed spreads within a configuration, the plane differs from the sealed one in modulus, training fraction, widths, step limit, and seeds, and no slope interval was computed; we therefore draw no inference from them.\n230\t% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#search]]\n231\t\n232\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n233\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n234\t\n235\t\\paragraph{Relation to the explanations of grokking.} Each account in Section~\\ref{sec:related} associates the delay with a quantity that can depend on width or parametrization: initial weight norm and the loss landscape, the relative efficiency of memorizing and generalizing circuits, and the position on the lazy-to-rich axis. The evaluated model touches the last two knobs directly: its initial weights are shrunk by half, which the source comments describe as aimed at reducing lazy-regime memorization, and its multipliers change how large a step each layer takes at a given width. A clear width slope from this model could therefore reflect the multipliers rather than width alone, and a flat slope could reflect their compensating a stock-model dependence. This is why the isolating comparison with a matched stock model (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}, item iv) matters more than a further estimate of the slope for the evaluated model alone.\n236\t\n237\t\\paragraph{Alternative readings that the data cannot separate.} The interval is compatible with four readings (Table~\\ref{tab:readings}). (a) \\emph{Little or no width dependence} over $16$--$256$ in this setting. (b) \\emph{A real negative dependence masked by censoring and noise}: cap-compressed delays and four seeds could produce a flat-looking slope. (c) \\emph{Compensation by the width-aware multipliers}: if the hidden and output multipliers equalize effective step sizes across width, a dependence present in the stock model could be reduced in the evaluated one; the single-seed reference slope ($-0.368$) is more negative than the point estimate, but it is one seed without an interval and does not by itself support this reading. (d) \\emph{Dependence that is non-monotone in $\\ln d$}, which a linear slope cannot register. Each reading predicts something different about per-width delays and censoring flags, which are exactly the quantities not retained.\n238\t\n239\t\\begin{table}[t]\n240\t\\centering\n241\t\\caption{What each reading of the interval would imply for quantities that are not retained in the artifacts. None of these can be checked with the available records.}\n242\t\\label{tab:readings}\n243\t\\small\n244\t\\begin{tabular}{p{0.22\\textwidth}p{0.36\\textwidth}p{0.30\\textwidth}}\n245\t\\toprule\n246\tReading & Implication for per-width delays & Implication for censoring \\\\\n247\t\\midrule\n248\t(a) Little or no width dependence & Similar delays across widths, within seed noise & Censored runs not concentrated at either end \\\\\n249\t(b) Negative dependence masked by cap and noise & Longer delays at narrow widths, partly hidden at the cap & Censored runs concentrated at narrow widths \\\\\n250\t(c) Compensation by multipliers & Matched stock-model runs show a steeper slope than evaluated-model runs on the same seeds & Differs between the two models at the same widths \\\\\n251\t(d) Non-monotone in $\\ln d$ & Extremal delay at an intermediate width; contrast $\\beta$ near zero & Censoring at both ends or in the middle \\\\\n252\t\\bottomrule\n253\t\\end{tabular}\n254\t\\end{table}\n255\t\n256\t\\paragraph{Threshold and time-grid sensitivity.} The delay is read off a $250$-step grid with a $0.99$ threshold on both accuracies, so it is blind to differences below one probe interval and sensitive to where a run plateaus. The development evaluation shows how this can matter: all six runs of one configuration were censored even though their mean final held-out accuracy was $0.944$ (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}), and the retained records do not show whether that censoring came from a missed training threshold or a missed held-out threshold, or how close individual runs came to $0.99$. We did not vary the threshold or the grid in the sealed evaluation, so we cannot say how the slope would change under, for example, a $0.95$ threshold, and the retained artifacts do not allow it to be recomputed.\n257\t\n258\t\\paragraph{What would resolve it.} Without claiming these were performed: (i) report per-width, per-seed delays with explicit censoring flags and the reason for censoring (not fitting vs.\\ not generalizing); (ii) analyze delays as censored data instead of imputing the cap (survival-analysis regression, e.g.\\ \\citealp{Cox1972RegressionMA}, handles right-censored outcomes directly, although we did not apply it), or raise the step limit until few runs are censored; (iii) use more seeds, so that the bootstrap has more than $35$ distinct resamples; (iv) run the multipliers' exponent at $0$ and $0.5$ under the same protocol to isolate their effect (no result for such a sweep exists in the retained artifacts); (v) vary the training fraction, the modulus, and the weight decay, which determine the delay in the accounts reviewed in Section~\\ref{sec:related}; and (vi) record fitting and generalization steps separately to test the capacity-based reading directly.\n259\t\n260\t\\begin{table}[t]\n261\t\\centering\n262\t\\caption{Claims and their status under this study's evidence.}\n263\t\\label{tab:claims}\n264\t\\small\n265\t\\begin{tabular}{p{0.46\\textwidth}p{0.17\\textwidth}p{0.25\\textwidth}}\n266\t\\toprule\n267\tClaim & Status & Basis \\\\\n268\t\\midrule\n269\tDelay decreases with width in this setting & Not established & Interval $[-0.585,0.354]$ contains $0$ \\\\\n270\tDelay is independent of width in this setting & Not established & Same interval also contains slopes down to $-0.585$ \\\\\n271\tThe measurement was valid & Supported & Completed evaluation, controls $0.012$ vs.\\ ceiling $0.10$, no missing episodes \\\\\n272\tA substantial share of runs is censored & Supported & $8$ of $20$ runs \\\\\n273\tThe multipliers weaken or remove a width dependence & Not tested & Reference slope has one seed, no interval, no matched ablation \\\\\n274\tFitting time and generalization time scale differently with width & Not testable & Not retained in the artifacts \\\\\n275\tFindings carry over to other moduli, fractions, operations & Not tested & One configuration only \\\\\n276\t\\bottomrule\n277\t\\end{tabular}\n278\t\\end{table}\n279\t\n280\t\\paragraph{Limitations.} Table~\\ref{tab:claims} summarizes which claims the evidence does and does not support. The evidence concerns one operation, one modulus ($97$), one training fraction ($0.4$), one optimizer setting, a single layer, and widths $16$--$256$ (up to $0.84\\times 10^6$ parameters). The evaluated model contains three simultaneous modifications relative to the stock model, so even a clear slope would not be attributable to any one of them; sequential changes made during development support at most comparisons between whole configurations. The reference slope has one seed. The seed-cluster interval rests on four seeds. Censoring merges two events. The development-plane numbers come from a different workload with two seeds. We did not test the explanations in Section~\\ref{sec:related}, did not run any weight-norm, circuit-level, or lazy-to-rich diagnostics, and did not run ablations of the three modifications. A valid evaluation does not imply scientific support for the prediction; the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}, and the design's limits above, rather than a demonstrated absence of effect, are the most defensible reading.\n281\t\n282\t\\section{Conclusion}\n283\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n284\tWe tested whether the grokking delay of a one-layer transformer on modular addition decreases with width, using a censoring-aware slope test with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval. The sealed evaluation was valid and gave a slope of $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$ over widths $16$--$256$ and four seeds, so the prediction is neither supported nor refuted. Forty percent of runs were censored at the step limit, the interval covers about two-fifths of the slopes that the delay floor and cap allow, and a four-cluster bootstrap has only $35$ distinct resamples. A single-seed reference trainer gave $-0.368$, inside the interval. We therefore do not claim that delay depends on width, nor that it does not, and we do not attribute the difference between the reference and the evaluated model to any of its modifications. The practical lesson is a measurement one: for a delay statistic with a floor and a censoring cap, report per-width delays and censoring flags, use enough seeds that the cluster bootstrap has a rich resampling support, and treat the attainable slope range as part of the result. The per-width records, the isolating ablation of the multipliers, and sweeps over training fraction and weight decay would turn this inconclusive result into a testable one.\n285\t\n286\t\\clearpage\n287\t\\bibliography{references}\n288\t\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n289\t\n290\t\\clearpage\n291\t\\appendix\n292\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n293\t\n294\t\\subsection{Protocol and hyperparameters}\n295\t\\label{sec:si-protocol}\n296\tTable~\\ref{tab:si-protocol} lists the workloads of the sealed evaluation and of the development evaluation, as specified in the evaluator's workload manifests (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/workload/}).\n297\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/workload/search_manifest.json]]\n298\t\n299\t\\begin{table}[h]\n300\t\\centering\n301\t\\caption{Workloads. The evaluator owns all items; the evaluated model receives only the training rows.}\n302\t\\label{tab:si-protocol}\n303\t\\small\n304\t\\begin{tabular}{lll}\n305\t\\toprule\n306\t & Sealed final evaluation & Development evaluation \\\\\n307\t\\midrule\n308\tOperation, modulus $p$ & addition, $97$ & addition, $53$ \\\\\n309\tTraining fraction & $0.4$ ($3{,}763$ pairs) & $0.5$ \\\\\n310\tWidths $d$ & $16,32,64,128,256$ & $32,64,128$ \\\\\n311\tSeeds & $201$--$204$ & $101,102$ \\\\\n312\tStep limit / probe interval & $10{,}000$ / $250$ & $6{,}000$ / $300$ \\\\\n313\tControl width & $64$ & $64$ \\\\\n314\tOptimizer & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{AdamW, lr $10^{-3}$, $\\beta=(0.9,0.98)$, weight decay $1.0$, full batch} \\\\\n315\tHeads, layers & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$4$, $1$} \\\\\n316\tFit / generalization threshold & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$0.99$ accuracy} \\\\\n317\tBootstrap (final only) & $2{,}000$ seed-cluster resamples & -- \\\\\n318\t\\bottomrule\n319\t\\end{tabular}\n320\t\\end{table}\n321\t\n322\tThe final evaluation made $1{,}804$ calls to the evaluated model (limit $2{,}200$; $22$ episodes, each a reset, a data load, and $40$ train/predict pairs) and took $11{,}764$\\,s of wall-clock time including the reference trainer.\n323\t% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#compute]] @evidence[[source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json#compute_usage]]\n324\t\n325\t\\subsection{Evaluated model: constants and parameter counts}\n326\t\\label{sec:si-model}\n327\tTable~\\ref{tab:si-model} gives the width-dependent constants and parameter counts of the evaluated model. The parameter count follows from the layer shapes in the source: embeddings $(p+1)d$, positional embedding $3d$, attention projections $3d^2+d^2$ (no bias), MLP $4d^2+4d$ and $4d^2+d$, unembedding $pd$ (no bias), totalling $12d^2+(2p+9)d$. The initial-weight scale ($0.5$), the unembedding scale ($0.5$), the reference width ($128$), and both exponents ($0.5$) are constants in the source; the code comments state they were chosen without offline tuning.\n328\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#params_by_width]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#hidden_and_output_multipliers]]\n329\t\n330\t\\begin{table}[h]\n331\t\\centering\n332\t\\caption{Parameter count and multiplier $m_d=(128/d)^{1/2}$ by width.}\n333\t\\label{tab:si-model}\n334\t\\small\n335\t\\begin{tabular}{rrr}\n336\t\\toprule\n337\tWidth $d$ & Parameters & $m_d$ \\\\\n338\t\\midrule\n339\t16 & 6{,}320 & 2.83 \\\\\n340\t32 & 18{,}784 & 2.00 \\\\\n341\t64 & 62{,}144 & 1.41 \\\\\n342\t128 & 222{,}592 & 1.00 \\\\\n343\t256 & 838{,}400 & 0.71 \\\\\n344\t\\bottomrule\n345\t\\end{tabular}\n346\t\\end{table}\n347\t\n348\t\\subsection{Development-plane comparison (descriptive, not final)}\n349\t\\label{sec:si-dev}\n350\tFigure~\\ref{fig:dev} shows the three development configurations: V1 (smaller initial weights and last-position-only computation), V2 (V1 plus width-aware output scaling), and V3 (V2 plus hidden-layer reparametrization; the evaluated model). Scores are means over two seeds on the development plane; per-seed scores are plotted. Table~\\ref{tab:si-dev} adds the mean final held-out accuracy and the censoring counts. The rank order by score is reversed relative to the end-of-training accuracy: the mean final held-out accuracy decreases from V1 to V3 ($0.981$, $0.944$, $0.921$) while the score increases ($0.798$, $0.828$, $0.850$), so the score and the end-of-training accuracy measure different things. All six V2 runs are censored although their mean final held-out accuracy is $0.944$; the retained records do not show whether the censoring came from a missed training threshold or a missed held-out threshold, or how close individual runs came to $0.99$. The lower V3 seed ($0.801$) lies just above the V1 mean ($0.798$), while the higher V3 seed ($0.899$) lies above the V2 mean ($0.828$). With two seeds per configuration, the seed-to-seed spreads ($0.051$, $0.043$, $0.098$) are no smaller than the differences between configuration means ($0.031$, $0.022$), so no ordering of the configurations is supported.\n351\t% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#search]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant1_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant2_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant3_metrics.json]]\n352\t\n353\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n354\t\\centering\n355\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/search_variants_descriptive.pdf}\n356\t\\caption{Development-plane comparison of three configurations (modulus $53$, training fraction $0.5$, two seeds; not the sealed evaluation). (a) Per-seed and mean held-out-accuracy AUC. (b) Number of the six runs censored at the step limit.}\n357\t\\label{fig:dev}\n358\t\\end{figure}\n359\t\n360\t\\begin{table}[h]\n361\t\\centering\n362\t\\caption{Development-plane summary (two seeds $\\times$ three widths $=6$ runs per configuration).}\n363\t\\label{tab:si-dev}\n364\t\\small\n365\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n366\t\\toprule\n367\tConfiguration & Score & Mean final held-out acc. & Censored runs & Control max acc. \\\\\n368\t\\midrule\n369\tV1 & $0.798$ & $0.981$ & $4/6$ & $0.023$ \\\\\n370\tV2 & $0.828$ & $0.944$ & $6/6$ & $0.022$ \\\\\n371\tV3 (evaluated) & $0.850$ & $0.921$ & $4/6$ & $0.023$ \\\\\n372\t\\bottomrule\n373\t\\end{tabular}\n374\t\\end{table}\n375\t\n376\t\\subsection{Analyses and hypotheses not performed}\n377\t\\label{sec:si-untested}\n378\tThe following were considered during development and have \\emph{no} results in the artifacts: a capacity-crossover account in which fitting time falls faster with width than generalization time (separate fitting and generalization steps are not retained); a critical width as a function of training fraction; a sweep of the hidden-layer exponent; a sweep of the output-scaling exponent and unembedding scale; an intervention on weight decay; and any weight-norm or circuit-level diagnostics. Nothing in the main text depends on them.\n379\t\n380\t\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n381\t\\label{sec:si-repro}\n382\tThe accompanying files are: the evaluated model's source and the two evaluators (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/}); the aggregate and per-seed final records (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/final/}); \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}, which computes every derived number in Tables~\\ref{tab:resolution} and~\\ref{tab:si-model} and the text from the evaluator records and writes \\texttt{derived/resolution.json}; and \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py}, which produces Figures~\\ref{fig:slope} and~\\ref{fig:dev} from that file. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data. Data are generated in memory from the modulus and seed, with no download. The aggregate metrics (slope, interval, reference slope, censored fraction, control, score) are those recorded by the evaluator. Re-running the evaluator requires the evaluated model's dependencies (PyTorch and NumPy); the sealed evaluation took about $3.3$ hours of wall-clock time on the recorded hardware, whose specification is not retained in the artifacts.\n383\t\n384\t\\subsection{LLM usage}\n385\t\\label{sec:si-llm}\n386\tLarge language models acted as autonomous agents in this work: they proposed the candidate directions, wrote and modified the evaluated model's code, drafted the analysis scripts, and wrote this manuscript. The task environment supplied the evaluators and the workload manifests; the models did not edit them. The interval rule that maps the evaluator's reported interval to a verdict was applied by our own script \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}. No results were produced by an LLM other than by executing the code described above, and all numbers in the paper come from the evaluator records or from the derivations in \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}.\n387\t\n388\t\\end{document}\n389\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90325347-1de7-4675-a6c9-9716e2cbade7","uuid":"afebf88b-85e7-406f-a93d-66809a147682","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:54:24.003Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper.tex","content":"An inconclusive verdict is informative only relative to the effects the design could have resolved. Three properties of the protocol, all computed from its constants and the reported interval (Table~\\ref{tab:resolution}), limit that resolution.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json#full]]\n\n\\emph{Bounded dynamic range.} In this balanced design the slope is a fixed linear contrast of the width-wise mean log-delays $m_d=\\tfrac14\\sum_s\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\beta=\\sum_{d} w_d\\,m_d,\\qquad (w_{16},\\dots,w_{256})=(-0.2,\\,-0.1,\\,0,\\,0.1,\\,0.2),\n\\end{equation}\nthat is, $\\beta=0.2\\,(m_{256}-m_{16})+0.1\\,(m_{128}-m_{32})$; the middle width carries no weight, and the verdict depends only on how the two widest models compare with the two narrowest. Because every $m_d\\in[\\ln 250,\\ln 10{,}000]$, $|\\beta|\\le 0.3\\ln 40=1.107$, attained by a step profile; a delay falling log-linearly from the cap at $d=16$ to the floor at $d=256$ would give $0.922$. The interval has width $0.939$ and covers $42\\%$ of the attainable range $[-1.107,1.107]$. Its lower arm extends $0.565$ below the point estimate and its upper arm $0.374$ above.\n\n\\emph{Distance to a supported verdict.} Keeping the upper arm of the interval unchanged, a verdict of supported would have required a point estimate below $-0.374$. The reference slope ($-0.368$) is slightly above that value; with the same upper arm it would give an upper endpoint of $+0.006$, still straddling zero. This is arithmetic on the observed spread, not a forecast: the spread of a different point estimate would differ.\n\n\\emph{A coarse bootstrap.} With four seeds there are only $\\binom{7}{4}=35$ distinct multisets of seeds to resample, and a fraction $4/4^4=1/64$ of draws consist of a single seed repeated four times. The $2{,}000$ resamples therefore estimate a discrete distribution with at most $35$ support points; the percentile endpoints $-0.585$ and $0.354$ should be read as coarse, and the coverage of an interval built from four clusters is not guaranteed to be nominal.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Quantities derived from the protocol constants and the reported interval (not additional measurements).}\n\\label{tab:resolution}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nQuantity & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nDelay floor / cap (steps) & $250$ / $10{,}000$ \\\\\nLargest attainable $|\\text{slope}|$ (step profile) & $1.107$ \\\\\nSlope of a log-linear cap-to-floor profile & $0.922$ \\\\\nInterval width & $0.939$ \\\\\nInterval width as fraction of $[-1.107, 1.107]$ & $0.42$ \\\\\nInterval arms below / above the point estimate & $0.565$ / $0.374$ \\\\\nPer-doubling delay factor: point [interval] & $0.980$ [$0.557$, $1.425$] \\\\\n$16\\to256$ delay factor: point [interval] & $0.924$ [$0.096$, $4.12$] \\\\\n$16\\to256$ delay factor at the reference slope & $0.230$ \\\\\nDistinct bootstrap seed multisets & $35$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Censoring}\nForty percent of the $20$ runs ($8$ runs) were censored at the step limit and entered the regression with the cap value $10{,}000$. Censoring has two consequences. First, because censored delays are replaced by the largest permitted value, any real dependence whose long-delay end is censored is compressed, so the fitted slope is biased toward zero when long delays concentrate at one end of the width range. Second, \\emph{censored} here covers two different events, held-out accuracy never reaching $0.99$ and training accuracy never reaching $0.99$; the retained artifacts do not say which occurred or at which widths, so they cannot separate delayed generalization from failure to fit. The score in Table~\\ref{tab:metrics} offers a threshold-free view: averaged over widths and seeds, held-out accuracy integrated over the $40$ checkpoints is $0.729$, so the runs on average generalize to a substantial degree within the window, but this aggregate does not locate where in the width range that happens.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.censored_fraction]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#n_censored_runs]]\n\n\\subsection{Seed-level variability}\nThe four seeds differ considerably in the score (Table~\\ref{tab:seeds}), from $0.607$ to $0.860$ with mean $0.729$ and sample standard deviation $0.104$. We report this as variability across seeds, not as a performance comparison: each seed's score averages five widths, and a single seed supplies one run at every width. That the seed-to-seed spread is large relative to the quantities of interest is consistent with the wide interval for the slope, although the artifacts do not allow a variance decomposition across widths.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#seed_score_sd]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Seed-level records from the sealed evaluation. Score: mean over the five widths of the held-out-accuracy AUC. Wall-clock time is summed over all episodes with that seed; seed 201 also includes the two control runs.}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n\\toprule\nSeed & Score & Wall-clock (s) \\\\\n\\midrule\n201 & $0.607$ & $2{,}468$ \\\\\n202 & $0.733$ & $1{,}894$ \\\\\n203 & $0.717$ & $2{,}062$ \\\\\n204 & $0.860$ & $1{,}943$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nMean $\\pm$ s.d. & $0.729 \\pm 0.104$ & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Cross-check against the reference trainer}\nThe reference trainer, run on seed $201$ only, gives a slope of $-0.368$, a fitted $16\\to256$ delay factor of $0.230$, and lies inside the interval, $23\\%$ of the way from its lower endpoint to its upper. The evaluated model's point estimate is $0.348$ larger (less negative). We draw no conclusion about this difference: the reference slope is one seed with no uncertainty, five points define it, and the evaluated model differs from the reference in three simultaneous ways (Section~\\ref{sec:model}). The comparison also does not establish that the multipliers weaken the width dependence: that would require both variants to be run with several seeds under the same protocol, which was not done.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.reference_delay_log_slope]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#reference_position_in_ci_fraction_from_low]]\n\n\\subsection{Validity checks and development-plane context}\nThe two controls stayed far below their ceiling (maximum held-out accuracy $0.012$ against a ceiling of $0.10$), no episodes were missing, and the evaluator reports a completed, valid evaluation. These checks support the integrity of the measurement. They do not bear on the width question, and a valid measurement can be inconclusive.\n\nFor context, three configurations that differ cumulatively by the three modifications of Section~\\ref{sec:model} were evaluated on the separate development plane with two seeds each, with scores $0.798$, $0.828$, and $0.850$ and $4$, $6$, and $4$ of $6$ runs censored (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}). Differences between configuration means are no larger than the seed-to-seed spreads within a configuration, the plane differs from the sealed one in modulus, training fraction, widths, step limit, and seeds, and no slope interval was computed; we therefore draw no inference from them.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#search]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\n\\paragraph{Relation to the explanations of grokking.} Each account in Section~\\ref{sec:related} associates the delay with a quantity that can depend on width or parametrization: initial weight norm and the loss landscape, the relative efficiency of memorizing and generalizing circuits, and the position on the lazy-to-rich axis. The evaluated model touches the last two knobs directly: its initial weights are shrunk by half, which the source comments describe as aimed at reducing lazy-regime memorization, and its multipliers change how large a step each layer takes at a given width. A clear width slope from this model could therefore reflect the multipliers rather than width alone, and a flat slope could reflect their compensating a stock-model dependence. This is why the isolating comparison with a matched stock model (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}, item iv) matters more than a further estimate of the slope for the evaluated model alone.\n\n\\paragraph{Alternative readings that the data cannot separate.} The interval is compatible with four readings (Table~\\ref{tab:readings}). (a) \\emph{Little or no width dependence} over $16$--$256$ in this setting. (b) \\emph{A real negative dependence masked by censoring and noise}: cap-compressed delays and four seeds could produce a flat-looking slope. (c) \\emph{Compensation by the width-aware multipliers}: if the hidden and output multipliers equalize effective step sizes across width, a dependence present in the stock model could be reduced in the evaluated one; the single-seed reference slope ($-0.368$) is more negative than the point estimate, but it is one seed without an interval and does not by itself support this reading. (d) \\emph{Dependence that is non-monotone in $\\ln d$}, which a linear slope cannot register. Each reading predicts something different about per-width delays and censoring flags, which are exactly the quantities not retained.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{What each reading of the interval would imply for quantities that are not retained in the artifacts. None of these can be checked with the available records.}\n\\label{tab:readings}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{p{0.22\\textwidth}p{0.36\\textwidth}p{0.30\\textwidth}}\n\\toprule\nReading & Implication for per-width delays & Implication for censoring \\\\\n\\midrule\n(a) Little or no width dependence & Similar delays across widths, within seed noise & Censored runs not concentrated at either end \\\\\n(b) Negative dependence masked by cap and noise & Longer delays at narrow widths, partly hidden at the cap & Censored runs concentrated at narrow widths \\\\\n(c) Compensation by multipliers & Matched stock-model runs show a steeper slope than evaluated-model runs on the same seeds & Differs between the two models at the same widths \\\\\n(d) Non-monotone in $\\ln d$ & Extremal delay at an intermediate width; contrast $\\beta$ near zero & Censoring at both ends or in the middle \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Threshold and time-grid sensitivity.} The delay is read off a $250$-step grid with a $0.99$ threshold on both accuracies, so it is blind to differences below one probe interval and sensitive to where a run plateaus. The development evaluation shows how this can matter: all six runs of one configuration were censored even though their mean final held-out accuracy was $0.944$ (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}), and the retained records do not show whether that censoring came from a missed training threshold or a missed held-out threshold, or how close individual runs came to $0.99$. We did not vary the threshold or the grid in the sealed evaluation, so we cannot say how the slope would change under, for example, a $0.95$ threshold, and the retained artifacts do not allow it to be recomputed.\n\n\\paragraph{What would resolve it.} Without claiming these were performed: (i) report per-width, per-seed delays with explicit censoring flags and the reason for censoring (not fitting vs.\\ not generalizing); (ii) analyze delays as censored data instead of imputing the cap (survival-analysis regression, e.g.\\ \\citealp{Cox1972RegressionMA}, handles right-censored outcomes directly, although we did not apply it), or raise the step limit until few runs are censored; (iii) use more seeds, so that the bootstrap has more than $35$ distinct resamples; (iv) run the multipliers' exponent at $0$ and $0.5$ under the same protocol to isolate their effect (no result for such a sweep exists in the retained artifacts); (v) vary the training fraction, the modulus, and the weight decay, which determine the delay in the accounts reviewed in Section~\\ref{sec:related}; and (vi) record fitting and generalization steps separately to test the capacity-based reading directly.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Claims and their status under this study's evidence.}\n\\label{tab:claims}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{p{0.46\\textwidth}p{0.17\\textwidth}p{0.25\\textwidth}}\n\\toprule\nClaim & Status & Basis \\\\\n\\midrule\nDelay decreases with width in this setting & Not established & Interval $[-0.585,0.354]$ contains $0$ \\\\\nDelay is independent of width in this setting & Not established & Same interval also contains slopes down to $-0.585$ \\\\\nThe measurement was valid & Supported & Completed evaluation, controls $0.012$ vs.\\ ceiling $0.10$, no missing episodes \\\\\nA substantial share of runs is censored & Supported & $8$ of $20$ runs \\\\\nThe multipliers weaken or remove a width dependence & Not tested & Reference slope has one seed, no interval, no matched ablation \\\\\nFitting time and generalization time scale differently with width & Not testable & Not retained in the artifacts \\\\\nFindings carry over to other moduli, fractions, operations & Not tested & One configuration only \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.} Table~\\ref{tab:claims} summarizes which claims the evidence does and does not support. The evidence concerns one operation, one modulus ($97$), one training fraction ($0.4$), one optimizer setting, a single layer, and widths $16$--$256$ (up to $0.84\\times 10^6$ parameters). The evaluated model contains three simultaneous modifications relative to the stock model, so even a clear slope would not be attributable to any one of them; sequential changes made during development support at most comparisons between whole configurations. The reference slope has one seed. The seed-cluster interval rests on four seeds. Censoring merges two events. The development-plane numbers come from a different workload with two seeds. We did not test the explanations in Section~\\ref{sec:related}, did not run any weight-norm, circuit-level, or lazy-to-rich diagnostics, and did not run ablations of the three modifications. A valid evaluation does not imply scientific support for the prediction; the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}, and the design's limits above, rather than a demonstrated absence of effect, are the most defensible reading.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\nWe tested whether the grokking delay of a one-layer transformer on modular addition decreases with width, using a censoring-aware slope test with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval. The sealed evaluation was valid and gave a slope of $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$ over widths $16$--$256$ and four seeds, so the prediction is neither supported nor refuted. Forty percent of runs were censored at the step limit, the interval covers about two-fifths of the slopes that the delay floor and cap allow, and a four-cluster bootstrap has only $35$ distinct resamples. A single-seed reference trainer gave $-0.368$, inside the interval. We therefore do not claim that delay depends on width, nor that it does not, and we do not attribute the difference between the reference and the evaluated model to any of its modifications. The practical lesson is a measurement one: for a delay statistic with a floor and a censoring cap, report per-width delays and censoring flags, use enough seeds that the cluster bootstrap has a rich resampling support, and treat the attainable slope range as part of the result. The per-width records, the isolating ablation of the multipliers, and sweeps over training fraction and weight decay would turn this inconclusive result into a testable one.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Protocol and hyperparameters}\n\\label{sec:si-protocol}\nTable~\\ref{tab:si-protocol} lists the workloads of the sealed evaluation and of the development evaluation, as specified in the evaluator's workload manifests (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/workload/}).\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/workload/search_manifest.json]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Workloads. The evaluator owns all items; the evaluated model receives only the training rows.}\n\\label{tab:si-protocol}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lll}\n\\toprule\n & Sealed final evaluation & Development evaluation \\\\\n\\midrule\nOperation, modulus $p$ & addition, $97$ & addition, $53$ \\\\\nTraining fraction & $0.4$ ($3{,}763$ pairs) & $0.5$ \\\\\nWidths $d$ & $16,32,64,128,256$ & $32,64,128$ \\\\\nSeeds & $201$--$204$ & $101,102$ \\\\\nStep limit / probe interval & $10{,}000$ / $250$ & $6{,}000$ / $300$ \\\\\nControl width & $64$ & $64$ \\\\\nOptimizer & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{AdamW, lr $10^{-3}$, $\\beta=(0.9,0.98)$, weight decay $1.0$, full batch} \\\\\nHeads, layers & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$4$, $1$} \\\\\nFit / generalization threshold & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$0.99$ accuracy} \\\\\nBootstrap (final only) & $2{,}000$ seed-cluster resamples & -- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe final evaluation made $1{,}804$ calls to the evaluated model (limit $2{,}200$; $22$ episodes, each a reset, a data load, and $40$ train/predict pairs) and took $11{,}764$\\,s of wall-clock time including the reference trainer.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#compute]] @evidence[[source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json#compute_usage]]\n\n\\subsection{Evaluated model: constants and parameter counts}\n\\label{sec:si-model}\nTable~\\ref{tab:si-model} gives the width-dependent constants and parameter counts of the evaluated model. The parameter count follows from the layer shapes in the source: embeddings $(p+1)d$, positional embedding $3d$, attention projections $3d^2+d^2$ (no bias), MLP $4d^2+4d$ and $4d^2+d$, unembedding $pd$ (no bias), totalling $12d^2+(2p+9)d$. The initial-weight scale ($0.5$), the unembedding scale ($0.5$), the reference width ($128$), and both exponents ($0.5$) are constants in the source; the code comments state they were chosen without offline tuning.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#params_by_width]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#hidden_and_output_multipliers]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Parameter count and multiplier $m_d=(128/d)^{1/2}$ by width.}\n\\label{tab:si-model}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{rrr}\n\\toprule\nWidth $d$ & Parameters & $m_d$ \\\\\n\\midrule\n16 & 6{,}320 & 2.83 \\\\\n32 & 18{,}784 & 2.00 \\\\\n64 & 62{,}144 & 1.41 \\\\\n128 & 222{,}592 & 1.00 \\\\\n256 & 838{,}400 & 0.71 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Development-plane comparison (descriptive, not final)}\n\\label{sec:si-dev}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:dev} shows the three development configurations: V1 (smaller initial weights and last-position-only computation), V2 (V1 plus width-aware output scaling), and V3 (V2 plus hidden-layer reparametrization; the evaluated model). Scores are means over two seeds on the development plane; per-seed scores are plotted. Table~\\ref{tab:si-dev} adds the mean final held-out accuracy and the censoring counts. The rank order by score is reversed relative to the end-of-training accuracy: the mean final held-out accuracy decreases from V1 to V3 ($0.981$, $0.944$, $0.921$) while the score increases ($0.798$, $0.828$, $0.850$), so the score and the end-of-training accuracy measure different things. All six V2 runs are censored although their mean final held-out accuracy is $0.944$; the retained records do not show whether the censoring came from a missed training threshold or a missed held-out threshold, or how close individual runs came to $0.99$. The lower V3 seed ($0.801$) lies just above the V1 mean ($0.798$), while the higher V3 seed ($0.899$) lies above the V2 mean ($0.828$). With two seeds per configuration, the seed-to-seed spreads ($0.051$, $0.043$, $0.098$) are no smaller than the differences between configuration means ($0.031$, $0.022$), so no ordering of the configurations is supported.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#search]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant1_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant2_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant3_metrics.json]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/search_variants_descriptive.pdf}\n\\caption{Development-plane comparison of three configurations (modulus $53$, training fraction $0.5$, two seeds; not the sealed evaluation). (a) Per-seed and mean held-out-accuracy AUC. (b) Number of the six runs censored at the step limit.}\n\\label{fig:dev}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-plane summary (two seeds $\\times$ three widths $=6$ runs per configuration).}\n\\label{tab:si-dev}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score & Mean final held-out acc. & Censored runs & Control max acc. \\\\\n\\midrule\nV1 & $0.798$ & $0.981$ & $4/6$ & $0.023$ \\\\\nV2 & $0.828$ & $0.944$ & $6/6$ & $0.022$ \\\\\nV3 (evaluated) & $0.850$ & $0.921$ & $4/6$ & $0.023$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Analyses and hypotheses not performed}\n\\label{sec:si-untested}\nThe following were considered during development and have \\emph{no} results in the artifacts: a capacity-crossover account in which fitting time falls faster with width than generalization time (separate fitting and generalization steps are not retained); a critical width as a function of training fraction; a sweep of the hidden-layer exponent; a sweep of the output-scaling exponent and unembedding scale; an intervention on weight decay; and any weight-norm or circuit-level diagnostics. Nothing in the main text depends on them.\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n\\label{sec:si-repro}\nThe accompanying files are: the evaluated model's source and the two evaluators (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/}); the aggregate and per-seed final records (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/final/}); \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}, which computes every derived number in Tables~\\ref{tab:resolution} and~\\ref{tab:si-model} and the text from the evaluator records and writes \\texttt{derived/resolution.json}; and \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py}, which produces Figures~\\ref{fig:slope} and~\\ref{fig:dev} from that file. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data. Data are generated in memory from the modulus and seed, with no download. The aggregate metrics (slope, interval, reference slope, censored fraction, control, score) are those recorded by the evaluator. Re-running the evaluator requires the evaluated model's dependencies (PyTorch and NumPy); the sealed evaluation took about $3.3$ hours of wall-clock time on the recorded hardware, whose specification is not retained in the artifacts.\n\n\\subsection{LLM usage}\n\\label{sec:si-llm}\nLarge language models acted as autonomous agents in this work: they proposed the candidate directions, wrote and modified the evaluated model's code, drafted the analysis scripts, and wrote this manuscript. The task environment supplied the evaluators and the workload manifests; the models did not edit them. The interval rule that maps the evaluator's reported interval to a verdict was applied by our own script \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}. No results were produced by an LLM other than by executing the code described above, and all numbers in the paper come from the evaluator records or from the derivations in \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}.\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":232,"startLine":158,"totalLines":389}}}
